"Internal Error ()\nciteturn77view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://wiki.aination.center/wiki/Синаполис/Agent_Tokenization_Verification_Model_v0\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://wiki.aination.center/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%81/Agent_Tokenization_Verification_Model_v0: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgent Work Receipt (https://agentworkreceipt.com/)\nciteturn77view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://agentworkreceipt.com/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 625\nL0: 【0†Agent Work Receipt】\nL1: \nL2: Runtime trust infrastructure for AI agents\nL3: # Prove the agent work. Then bill it.\nL4: \nL5: Agent Work Receipt creates a durable, evidence-backed receipt of what an agent did, what authorized the work, what evidence supports it, and whether it met the agreed terms, so the work can be reviewed, billed, audited, settled, disputed, scored, or handed off with confidence.\nL6: \nL7: 【1†Ship your first receipt†identity.usehello.ai】【2†Book a review】\nL8: \nL9: Platform-neutral. Built for regulated and high-accountability workflows.\nL10: \nL11: Proof for the agent economy\nL12: ## Evidence-backed billing\nL13: \nL14: The Agent Work Receipt is evidence that work happened, was verified against policy, and is exactly what you are billed for. Every line item traces to its own proof-of-work receipt.\nL15: \nL16: Voice is one example. Wherever AI does work, agents, assistants, copilots, or automations, it gets an Agent Work Receipt.\nL17: \nL18:   * 【3†Image】\nL19: \nL20: ### AI voice agent\nL21: \nL22: Answers and acts for your practice, 24/7\nL23: \nL24:   * 【4†Image】\nL25: ### Work\nL26: \nL27: Workflows and tasks the agent completes\nL28: \nL29:     * Appointment booked\nL30:     * Payment collected\nL31:     * Patient intake\nL32:   * 【5†Image】\nL33: \nL34: ### Agent Work System\nL35: \nL36: Verifies the work, checks compliance, and issues a sealed receipt\nL37: \nL38:   * 【6†Image】\nL39: ### Safe billing\nL40: \nL41: Every line traces to its receipt. Verifiable and undisputed.\nL42: \nL43: Agent Work Receipt\nL44: \nL45: Agent Work System\nL46: \nL47: Policy pass Evidence complete Billability earned\nL48: \nL49: Receipt ID awr_rcpt_8f2c41d9e7\nL50: \nL51: Type validated_work_event\nL52: \nL53: Lifecycle state earned\nL54: \nL55: Work type Workflows and Tasks\nL56: \nL57: Event appointment_booked_with_deposit\nL58: \nL59: Policy result pass\nL60: \nL61: Evidence 5 of 5\nL62: \nL63: Charge\nL64: \nL65: Meter M1 · Trusted interaction\nL66: \nL67: $1.50 USD\nL68: \nL69: Evidence manifest (5 of 5)\nL70: \nL71: scheduler_appointment_id SHA-256 b1c7e9a3...\nL72: payment_processor_transaction_id SHA-256 9f3a4d7c...\nL73: \nL74: payment_capture_status SHA-256 2d5e7f01...\nL75: \nL76: confirmation_message_timestamp SHA-256 7a6d2c3f...\nL77: \nL78: policy_scan_result SHA-256 1e9b4c6d...\nL79: \nL80: Sealed Cryptographically signed\nL81: \nL82: Seal hash a8d2f6e9c7b1d4a0...\nL83: \nL84: Signature ed25519 4f6a2b7c...\nL85: \nL86: Retention until 2029-06-29 · 3 years\nL87: \nL88: Jurisdiction us-east-1\nL89: \nL90: 【7†Where this receipt ends up The monthly statement】\nL91: \nL92: Tamper-evident. Verifiable. Defensible. Built for audit, built for trust.\nL93: AWR starts with voice agents in US healthcare, a $5.4B to $8.5B market where every unanswered patient call is lost revenue. Every recovered one gets an Agent Work Receipt.\nL94: \nL95: Starting use cases\nL96: ## Where receipts earn their keep.\nL97: \nL98: Start with agents whose output changes a schedule, moves money, or ships code. AWR makes the work reviewable before it becomes a charge, decision, or liability.\nL99: \nL100: 【8†See how a receipt is created】\nL101: \nL102:   * 01\nL103: ### AI voice operations\nL104: \nL105: Receipt the work completed across patient, customer, and revenue-critical conversations.\nL106: \nL107:     * Appointments, rescheduling, and cancellations\nL108:     * Intake and insurance verification\nL109:     * Payment collection during booking\nL110:     * No-show recovery and human handoff\nL111: \nL112: completion consent handoff\nL113: \nL114:   * 02\nL115: ### Payment operations\nL116: \nL117: Create defensible records for agents that move money, correct errors, and resolve exceptions.\nL118: \nL119:     * Procurement and accounts payable\nL120:     * Refunds, reversals, and corrections\nL121:     * Disputes and chargebacks\nL122:     * Settlement and reconciliation\nL123: \nL124: authority exception settlement\nL125: \nL126:   * 03\nL127: ### PR Review\nL128: \nL129: Preserve the standards, findings, remediation, and judgment behind agent-assisted releases.\nL130: \nL131:     * Pull request review runs\nL132:     * Security, quality, and policy checks\nL133:     * Findings, remediation, and re-review\nL134:     * Approval and merge recommendations\nL135: \nL136: standards findings decision\nL137: \nL138: The proof gap\nL139: ## AI usage is measurable.AI value usually is not.\nL140: \nL141: You can meter AI consumption to the token. You still cannot reliably prove which AI work created value. The trust problem is no longer “did the model respond?” It is “did the agent actually complete the work, follow policy, and produce evidence we can defend in audit, dispute, or billing?”\nL142: \nL143: AI usage Unverified work\nL144: \nL145: AI value Trusted event\nL146: \nL147:   * AI slop\nL148:   * Retry cost\nL149:   * Duplicate work\nL150:   * Human correction\nL151:   * Rejected output\nL152: \nL153: Billed alike\nL154: AWR verifies the work\nL155: \nL156: Interaction Policy\nL157: \nL158: AWR\nL159: \nL160: Evidence Defensible billing\nL161: \nL162:   * duplicate_suppressed\nL163:   * failed_validation\nL164:   * non_billable\nL165: \nL166: AWR earned\nL167: \nL168: Billable\nL169: \nL170: AWR makes the trusted event the center of the system.\nL171: \nL172: 85%\nL173:     of organizations that can see their full AI cost still cannot demonstrate established ROI.\nL174: \nL175: 97%\nL176:     of organizations without that visibility cannot demonstrate it either.\nL177: Source: KPMG Global AI Pulse Q2 2026, 2,145 senior leaders across 20 countries. In the same survey, 35% report AI operating costs that are fully visible and actively monitored, 29% report difficulty controlling those costs as systems scale, and 40% have a usage or token budget in place.\nL178: \nL179: Stop measuring AI activity as productivity. Prove which AI work earns the right to scale.\nL180: \nL181: AWR turns “trust us” into “【7†here is the receipt】.”\nL182: \nL183: 【9†Run your own numbers】\nL184: \nL185: From action to invoice\nL186: ## Every charge must survive the proof.\nL187: \nL188: AWR captures the action, verifies its evidence and policy, decides billability, and carries only earned work into the customer statement.\nL189: \nL190: Capture\nL191: \nL192: 01–02\nL193: \nL194: 01\nL195: \nL196: ### Action captured\nL197: \nL198: received\nL199: \nL200: Source, payload, and execution context are retained exactly as received.\nL201: \nL202: 02\nL203: \nL204: ### Classified\nL205: \nL206: classified\nL207: \nL208: Work type, event key, and carried data classes are assigned.\nL209: \nL210: Verify\nL211: \nL212: 03–04\nL213: \nL214: 03\nL215: ### Evidence checked\nL216: \nL217: candidate_billable\nL218: \nL219: Required artifacts and duplicate conditions are checked against the rule.\nL220: \nL221: 04\nL222: \nL223: ### Policy applied\nL224: \nL225: validated\nL226: \nL227: Runtime policy produces a retained verdict, including every failure.\nL228: \nL229: Decide\nL230: \nL231: 05–06\nL232: \nL233: 05\nL234: \nL235: ### Billability decided\nL236: \nL237: earned\nL238: \nL239: One decision separates earned work from work that cannot be charged.\nL240: \nL241: Earned work continues.\nL242: \nL243: Everything else exits with a named reason.\nL244: \nL245: 06\nL246: ### Decision sealed\nL247: \nL248: posted_to_ledger\nL249: \nL250: The result is signed, retained, and independently verifiable.\nL251: \nL252: duplicate_suppressed · failed_validation · non_billable\nL253: \nL254: Bill it Proof becomes a charge.\nL255: \nL256: 07\nL257: \nL258: ### Statement assembled\nL259: \nL260: Earned work aggregates by billing period, with proof attached to every charge line.\nL261: \nL262: AWR prices it You price it\nL263: \nL264: 08\nL265: \nL266: ### Invoice handed off\nL267: \nL268: AWR boundary → payment provider\nL269: \nL270: 【7†See the billed statement】【1†Ship your first receipt†identity.usehello.ai】\nL271: \nL272: The receipt primitive\nL273: ## The product is the evidence object.\nL274: \nL275: An Agent Work Receipt is not a log, trace, audit trail, or usage record. Those are inputs. The receipt is the durable unit of truth that shows who acted, who authorized it, what work happened, what it cost, what evidence exists, whether it is billable, and whether it can be accepted, disputed, audited, exported, settled, scored, or handed off.\nL276: \nL277: 【1†Ship your first receipt†identity.usehello.ai】\nL278: \nL279: POST /v1/events 202 Accepted\nL280: \nL281: {\nL282:   \"event_key\": \"appointment_booked_with_deposit\",\nL283: \nL284:   \"agent_id\": \"agt_after_hours_care_agent\",\nL285: \nL286:   \"interaction_id\": \"int_01JZ7Z...\",\nL287: \nL288:   \"channel\": \"voice\",\nL289: \nL290:   \"occurred_at\": \"2026-05-19T17:22:31Z\",\nL291: \nL292:   \"idempotency_key\": \"appointment_booked_with_deposit:int_01JZ7Z...\",\nL293: \nL294:   \"evidence\": {\nL295: \nL296:     \"scheduler_appointment_id\": \"appt_12345\",\nL297: \nL298:     \"payment_transaction_id\": \"pi_12345\"\nL299: \nL300:   }\nL301: \nL302: }\nL303: \nL304: ↓You assert the event. AWR derives work type, tenancy, and billability, and seals the receipt.\nL305: GET /v1/work-receipts/awr_2479_health_voice_payment 200 OK\nL306: \nL307: {\nL308: \nL309:   \"receipt_id\": \"awr_2479_health_voice_payment\",\nL310: \nL311:   \"event_type\": [\"voice_call\", \"payment_reference\", \"record_update\"],\nL312: \nL313:   \"agent_id\": \"after_hours_care_agent\",\nL314: \nL315:   \"workspace\": \"beacon_dermatology\",\nL316: \nL317:   \"authority\": {\nL318: \nL319:     \"source\": \"patient_consent + workspace_policy\",\nL320: \nL321:     \"policy_pack\": \"HIPAA/PHI, PCI tokenized\"\nL322: \nL323:   },\nL324: \nL325:   \"task\": {\nL326: \nL327:     \"type\": \"appointment_booked_with_deposit\",\nL328: \nL329:     \"status\": \"completed\"\nL330: \nL331:   },\nL332:   \"evidence_manifest\": [\"transcript\", \"tool_call\", \"scheduler_write\", \"payment_ref\"],\nL333: \nL334:   \"policy_verdict\": \"allowed_by_policy\",\nL335: \nL336:   \"usage_cost_reference\": \"usage_line_89317\",\nL337: \nL338:   \"billability_state\": \"earned\",\nL339: \nL340:   \"dispute_state\": \"window_open\",\nL341: \nL342:   \"trust_score\": 92,\nL343: \nL344:   \"signature_hash\": \"ed25519:6b73...d91a\"\nL345: \nL346: }\nL347: \nL348: Of the receipt fields above, AWR’s published data model defines receipt_id, event_type, agent_id, workspace, authority, policy_pack and evidence_manifest today.\nL349: \nL350: What AWR does about it\nL351: ## AWR connects every AI cost to the work and outcome behind it.\nL352: \nL353: Stop measuring AI activity as productivity. Prove which AI work earns the right to scale.\nL354: \nL355: AWR evidence chain\nL356: \nL357: Evidence attached\nL358: \nL359:   * Retry cost\nL360:   * Duplicate work\nL361:   * Human correction\nL362:   * Rejected output\nL363:   * Unverified value\nL364: \nL365:   1. 01 AI cost Provider usage + infrastructure + licensing + retries + human review\nL366: \nL367:   2. 02 Work receipt Agent + task + authorization + evidence + completion state\nL368:   3. 03 Accepted outcome Verified + used + policy-compliant + not reversed\nL369: \nL370:   4. 04 Value statement Cost per outcome + net verified value + ROI confidence\nL371: \nL372: AWR Work Economics Statement\nL373: \nL374: Accepted work verified\nL375: \nL376: Cost / accepted outcome\nL377:     $3.82\nL378: \nL379: Net verified value\nL380:     $42.6K\nL381: \nL382: Evidence coverage\nL383:     94%\nL384: \nL385: Every figure traces to an accepted outcome and the evidence behind it.\nL386: \nL387: 【10†Explore AI economics】【9†Run your own work economics】\nL388: \nL389: What AWR proves\nL390: ## The proof is already attached to the work.\nL391: \nL392: Every agent event carries the facts needed to approve the outcome, challenge the charge, or defend both without reconstructing what happened.\nL393: \nL394: Supported review outcomes: Accept, Dispute, Audit, Bill.\nL395: \nL396: Accept/Dispute/Audit/Bill\nL397: \nL398: ### Work\nL399: \nL400:   1. 01\nL401: \nL402: #### Action performed\nL403: \nL404: What the agent actually did.\nL405: \nL406:   2. 02\nL407: \nL408: #### Completion state\nL409: \nL410: Whether it finished, failed, or stopped.\nL411: \nL412:   3. 03\nL413: \nL414: #### Supporting evidence\nL415: \nL416: What proves the work happened.\nL417: \nL418: ### Control\nL419: \nL420:   1. 04\nL421: #### Policy decision\nL422: \nL423: Which rules were checked and passed.\nL424: \nL425:   2. 05\nL426: \nL427: #### Data handling\nL428: \nL429: How sensitive data was classified and handled.\nL430: \nL431:   3. 06\nL432: \nL433: #### Defensibility\nL434: \nL435: Why the result can withstand review.\nL436: \nL437: ### Commercial record\nL438: \nL439:   1. 07\nL440: \nL441: #### Billability\nL442: \nL443: Whether the work qualifies to be charged.\nL444: \nL445:   2. 08\nL446: \nL447: #### Retention\nL448: \nL449: How long the evidence must be preserved.\nL450: \nL451:   3. 09\nL452: #### Access history\nL453: \nL454: Who viewed, retrieved, or used the record.\nL455: \nL456: When the work or charge is challenged, the proof is already there.\nL457: \nL458: No log reconstruction. No billing guesswork. No unsupported outcome.\nL459: \nL460: Evidence-backed by design\nL461: \nL462: Why this is different\nL463: ## Most systems see around the work. AWR proves the work.\nL464: \nL465: Logs, meters, guardrails, GRC systems, and archives each capture one part of an agent event. AWR binds the action, authority, evidence, policy result, and outcome into one verifiable runtime record.\nL466: \nL467: What the existing stack can tell you Fragmented signals\nL468: \nL469: ### Logs\nL470: \nL471: Activity occurred.\nL472: \nL473: ### Observability\nL474: \nL475: Something failed.\nL476: \nL477: ### Billing meters\nL478: \nL479: Usage was consumed.\nL480: \nL481: ### GRC tools\nL482: \nL483: Controls are documented.\nL484: \nL485: ### Archives\nL486: \nL487: Records were retained.\nL488: ### Guardrails\nL489: \nL490: An output was blocked.\nL491: \nL492: What AWR can prove Unified runtime record\nL493: \nL494:   1. 01\nL495: \nL496: The work was performed.\nL497: \nL498:   2. 02\nL499: \nL500: The agent was authorized.\nL501: \nL502:   3. 03\nL503: \nL504: The required evidence exists.\nL505: \nL506:   4. 04\nL507: \nL508: The applicable policy was evaluated.\nL509: \nL510:   5. 05\nL511: \nL512: The outcome can be defended.\nL513: \nL514:   6. 06\nL515: \nL516: The event can be audited, disputed, retained, or billed.\nL517: \nL518: Posture proves controls exist. AWR proves what the agent actually did.\nL519: Runtime proof captured while the work is happening, not reconstructed later at invoice time or audit time.\nL520: \nL521: Questions\nL522: ## Agent Work Receipt FAQ\nL523: \nL524: What is Agent Work Receipt?\nL525: \nL526: Agent Work Receipt, or AWR, is runtime trust infrastructure for AI-agent work. AWR creates evidence-backed records showing what an AI agent did, which systems and policies governed the work, what evidence supports the result, and whether any resulting charge has billing integrity.\nL527: \nL528: What is an agent work receipt?\nL529: An agent work receipt is a verifiable record of a specific unit of work performed by an AI agent. It can include the agent identity, work requested, actions taken, tools used, policy checks, evidence references, timestamps, costs, outcomes, and cryptographic verification data. The receipt is the artifact; AWR is the platform that issues it.\nL530: \nL531: Why do AI agents need receipts?\nL532: Logs and traces show technical activity, but businesses also need to know whether useful work occurred, whether the work followed policy, whether the outcome can be supported by evidence, and whether the associated charge is justified. AWR turns technical execution into an accountable business record.\nL533: \nL534: Is AWR an AI observability platform?\nL535: AWR is not merely an observability platform. Observability products primarily explain system behaviour through logs, traces, metrics and evaluations. AWR uses execution evidence to establish accountable work, policy conformance, chain of custody, outcome attribution and billing integrity. AWR complements logs, traces, evaluations and billing systems rather than replacing them.\nL536: \nL537: What is billing integrity for AI-agent work?\nL538: Billing integrity means that a charge can be traced to actual agent work, authorised usage, relevant evidence, agreed pricing rules, and a measurable outcome or service obligation. AWR helps identify unsupported, duplicated, failed or incorrectly attributed charges.\nL539: \nL540: Is AWR tied to a specific LLM or agent platform?\nL541: No. AWR is designed to be model-agnostic and vendor-agnostic. It can record work produced by proprietary models, open-weight models, voice-agent platforms, workflow systems, custom agents or mixed-provider architectures.\nL542: \nL543: 【11†More FAQs】\nL544: ## Your agent did the work. Give it a receipt.\nL545: \nL546: Bring an agent workflow. We map it to work types, evidence and policy, and show you the receipt it would produce.\nL547: \nL548: 【2†Book a review】【7†View a sample receipt】\nL549: \nL550: Agent Work Receipt\nL551: \nL552: Prove the agent work. Then bill it.\nL553: \nL554: Receipts\nL555:   * 【12†Work receipts】\nL556:   * 【12†Evidence manifest】\nL557:   * 【12†Decision receipts】\nL558:   * 【12†Verified work】\nL559:   * 【13†Proof behind the zero】\nL560:   * 【12†Sealing】\nL561:   * 【12†Archive】\nL562:   * 【12†Receipt identity】\nL563:   * 【12†Trust block】\nL564:   * 【14†Retention on receipt】\nL565: \nL566: By function\nL567: \nL568:   * 【15†Voice agents】\nL569:   * 【15†Code review】\nL570:   * 【15†Customer service】\nL571:   * 【15†Payments】\nL572:   * 【15†Scheduling】\nL573:   * 【15†Intake】\nL574: \nL575: Build\nL576: \nL577:   * 【12†Event ingestion】\nL578:   * 【12†Canonical envelope】\nL579:   * 【12†Idempotency】\nL580:   * 【12†Sandbox】\nL581:   * 【12†Webhooks】\nL582: \nL583: Company\nL584:   * 【16†About】\nL585:   * 【17†Pricing】\nL586:   * 【18†Contact】\nL587: \nL588: Classification\nL589: \nL590:   * 【12†Event ingestion】\nL591:   * 【12†Canonical envelope】\nL592:   * 【12†Work types】\nL593:   * 【12†Event keys】\nL594:   * 【12†Required evidence】\nL595:   * 【13†Billability states】\nL596:   * 【13†Dispositions】\nL597:   * 【13†Exclusion reasons】\nL598: \nL599: By buyer\nL600: \nL601:   * 【13†Operators】\nL602:   * 【13†Clients and payers】\nL603:   * 【15†Compliance】\nL604:   * 【13†Finance】\nL605:   * 【12†Engineering】\nL606: \nL607: Resources\nL608:   * 【19†How it models work】\nL609:   * 【13†Where cost leaves the chain】\nL610:   * 【7†A receipt-backed statement】\nL611:   * 【11†FAQ】\nL612:   * 【14†Claims discipline】\nL613:   * 【14†Security and retention】\nL614: \nL615: Support\nL616: \nL617:   * 【18†Talk to us】\nL618:   * 【16†Status】\nL619: \nL620: Demonstrations and example visuals are illustrative and synthetic. No real PHI, PAN, or PII is used.\nL621: \nL622: Code Pantry LLC DBA Agent Work Receipt · 1500 Grant Street, Suite R, Denver, CO 80203, United States · 【20†[email protected]】 · +1 314 972 3674\nL623: \nL624: awr_rcpt_\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe found 331 MCP tool-list changes in one week that only moved a date. Each one costs a prompt-cache write · AI News (https://ainews.gemivo.de/news/we-found-331-mcp-tool-list-changes-in-one-week-that-only-cmw03yqv3005)\nciteturn77view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ainews.gemivo.de/news/we-found-331-mcp-tool-list-changes-in-one-week-that-only-cmw03yqv3005\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 52\nL0: 【0†ai news】【0†Start】【1†News】【2†Coding & Dev-Tools】【3†KI-Videos】【4†Modelle】【5†Tipps】【6†Anhören】Di., 3. Nov.\nL1: # We found 331 MCP tool-list changes in one week that only moved a date. Each one costs a prompt-cache write\nL2: \nL3: A reader of our last post, Paul Irolla, who builds a token optimizer for coding agents, pointed out something we had only looked at as a correctness problem. A model request starts with the tool list. Change one byte of a tool definition and every prompt cache built on that request is gone, even if\nL4: \nL5: dev.to Dienstag 04:00 · 03.11.5 Min. Lesezeit Link kopieren\nL6: \nL7: 【7†Image†media2.dev.to】Bild: dev.to\nL8: Posted on Nov 3 • Originally published at modelmarket.dev\nL9: \nL10: A reader of our last post, Paul Irolla, who builds a token optimizer for coding agents, pointed out something we had only looked at as a correctness problem. A model request starts with the tool list. Change one byte of a tool definition and every prompt cache built on that request is gone, even if the change means nothing.\nL11: HISTOR, our public log of MCP tool definitions, records every change it sees, so we counted. In the week of 25 October to 1 November it recorded 10,712 changes to the tool lists of 4,491 MCP servers. 331 of them changed nothing but a date or a timestamp.\nL12: With Anthropic's API, the cached prefix is built in a fixed order: tools first, then the system prompt, then the messages. A change anywhere in the tools invalidates everything after it. Reading a cached prefix costs a tenth of the normal input price. Writing one costs 1.25 times the input price, or twice for the one-hour cache. So the same tool list costs about 12 times more on a miss than on a hit, and so does everything behind it.\nL13: Other providers discount cached input by different amounts, but the principle is the same: an identical prefix is cheap, a changed one is not.\nL14: Inside one session this mostly doesn't bite. Clients fetch tools/list when they connect and keep it until the server says the list changed. It bites between sessions. If you run many agent sessions with the same servers, the cached prefix carries over from one session to the next until a byte changes. A server that rewrites its definitions twice a day resets that for everyone who uses it.\nL15: For every change in the week, we took both stored tool lists, the one before and the one after, and compared them tool by tool, ignoring more and more at each step:\nL16: \nL17: The step at which the two lists become equal names the change:\nL18: HISTOR's digest sorts tools and keys, so a list that only changed order isn't recorded as a change at all, and doesn't appear here. And HISTOR sees at most one change per read, every 30 minutes to 6 hours. A server that rewrites its date every minute shows up as one change per read. The counts are a floor.\nL19: One server accounts for 237 of the 331. Its whats_new tool describes the latest improvement and the time the server last read its own feed: \"Live now (feed read 2026-10-28T08:24:54Z)\". Each read of the feed rewrites the tool list. The other 94 date-only changes come from 26 servers, in four patterns.\nL20: Telling the model what day it is. \"Today is 2026-10-28 (UTC) — never infer the date from the…\", \"Hoy es 2026-10-28\", \"Current date is 2026-10-28.\", \"Current server time: 2026-10-25T02:36:38Z (UTC). Use this as your…\" The instinct is sound: a model doesn't know the date, and a booking or scheduling tool needs it. But in the description, the date changes the tool list every day.\nL21: Freshness stamps. \"measured 2026-10-28\", \"indexed on 2026-10-26\", \"list prices verified 2026-10-27\", \"as of 2026-10-29\", \"Rules version 2026-10-28.1\". These tell the reader how current the data is. They change whenever the data does, which can be daily.\nL22: \nL23: Examples that move with the calendar. A booking server's input schema has an example start time that moves forward a day at a time: 2026-10-29T10:00:00-07:00, then 2026-10-30T10:00:00-07:00. Three servers from one publisher do this.\nL24: Defaults set at startup. A default for a title like \"Conversation 2026-10-26T21:47:21.670Z\", or start_date and end_date defaults with the time the server started. These are npm packages, so the date changes on every launch.\nL25: \nL26: Of the 2,119 fields that differ in these two classes, 1,963 are tool descriptions and 156 are in input schemas: defaults, examples and property descriptions.\nL27: The 134 changes that only moved a decimal number look like the same kind of noise. They aren't. About 126 of them are prices written into the description: \"sold per call … at 0.009027 USDC\", then 0.008588; \"Price $0.01/call\", then $0.05; one tool went from \"$1.00\" to \"$222.00\". Five are version strings, such as a rules engine going from 1.6.12 to 1.7.1.\nL28: A price change is a real change for an agent that pays per call, so it belongs in the tool list's history. But if the price moves every few hours, the description is a costly place to keep it. Every move resets the cache for every client, including the ones that will never call that tool. A price that floats is better quoted at call time. Paid MCP tools over x402 already get a priced offer in the HTTP 402 answer.\nL29: If you maintain an MCP server and want the model to know the date, or how fresh your data is, there are places that don't touch the tool list:\nL30: \nL31: None of this changes what the model can know. It only moves the moving parts out of the one block that every request shares.\nL32: If you build a client or a token optimizer, the per-server history is public. HISTOR's changes API, https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/changes, lists every recorded change with the digests of both sides and a field-by-field diff. A server that changed its definitions seven or more times in a week is a good candidate to fetch once per session and pin. 218 servers did that this week, and between them they made 3,661 changes, a third of the total.\nL33: The week's counts, the script and the examples behind this post are at https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/cache-churn-2026-11-01/.\nL34: \nL35: Disclosure: we build HISTOR. AI assistance was used for the analysis, the code and the writing. The counts come from HISTOR's production database, read-only, in a throw-away container.\nL36: \nL37: Quelle: dev.to (dev.to). 【8†Original öffnen†dev.to】\nL38: ## Mehr zu dem Thema\nL39: \nL40:   * 【9†Apple restricts dating apps and AI companions to users 18 and up TechCrunch · Dienstag 16:38 · 03.11.】\nL41:   * 【10†How to give an AI agent a record it can't rewrite dev.to · Dienstag 15:00 · 03.11.】\nL42:   * 【11†Snowflake expands agentic AI platform with business context and governance tools SiliconANGLE AI · Dienstag 15:00 · 03.11.】\nL43: ## KI-News anhören\nL44: \nL45: Das tägliche Briefing als Sprachaudio, gesprochen im Nachrichtensender-Stil.\nL46: \nL47: 【6†Zum Player】\nL48: \nL49: Gestern 22 Besucher · 22 Seitenaufrufe · heute 33 Besucher · 35 Seitenaufrufe bisher. Zahlen: Umami, cookielos.\nL50: \nL51: 【0†Start KI-News der letzten Tage】【1†News alle Meldungen im Feed】【4†Modelle aktuelle KI-Modelle】【5†Tipps Anleitungen für die Praxis】【12†Mehr Archiv, Einstellungen und Über】\n"
